# Stable metal-organic frameworks with high catalytic performance in the cycloaddition of CO2 with aziridines

Stable metal-organic frameworks with high catalytic performance in the cycloaddition of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ with aziridines
Xiao-Min Kang, Ying Shi, Chun-Shuai Cao & Bin Zhao
College of Chemistry and Key Laboratory of Advanced Energy Material Chemistry, Nankai University, Tianjin 300071, China
Based on the mixed ligands XN ( $4^{\prime}$ -(4-pyridine) $)4{,}2{^{\prime}}{\cdot}2{^{\prime}}{,}4{^{\prime\prime}}$ -terpyridine) and isophthalic acid (IPA), three new metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) $\{\mathrm{[}\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{(XN)}_{2}\mathrm{(IPA)}_{2}\mathrm{]}{\bullet}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M=Co}$ (1), Mn (2), Ni (3)) were assembled and structurally characterized, presenting 3D pillar-chain feature structures. Stability measurements demonstrate that these compounds possess high thermostability and can endure different organic solvents as well as various acid/base solutions with $\mathsf{p H}$ range of 1 to 14. Importantly, compounds 1–3 can serve as high-efficiency catalysts for the transformation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and aziridines to form high-value oxazolidinones under mild conditions, exhibiting excellent cyclicity at least five times.
MOFs, stability, catalysis, oxazolidinones, $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion
Citation: Kang XM, Shi Y, Cao CS, Zhao B. Stable metal-organic frameworks with high catalytic performance in the cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with aziridines. Sci China Chem, 2019, 62, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-018-9420-6
## 1 Introduction
With the rapid development of industry, more attention has focused on the energy and environmental issues. Air pollution as one of the main environmental problems is posing a threat to the nature and human health due to the overuse of fossil energy. Carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ , as the major greenhouse gas generated from industrial and human activities, should take the responsibility for the global warming, climate change and acid rain [1–3]. On the other hand, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can be applied in the organic synthesis widely acting as an abundant, nontoxic and cheap C1 building block [4,5]. Therefore, it is urgent and challenging for both effective capture and utilization of carbon oxide, and great efforts have been made in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation to form numerous high-value chemicals that are obtained from fossil fuel resources generally [6–8]. In recent years, as one of the effective strategies for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and sequestration, the cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with aziridines stands out from many catalyzed reactions due to the high reactivity, satisfactory atom efficiency and the generation of high-value products [9–16]. Particularly, the desired target oxazolidinones have extensive application as antibacterial pharmaceuticals in medicinal chemistry and intermediates as well as chiral auxiliaries in organic synthesis [17–19]. To the best of our knowledge, although various homogeneous catalysts including tetraalkylammonium salt, $\mathbf{a}$ -amino acids and alkali metal halide have been utilized with good catalytic activity, it is hard to separate the catalyst and product [20–22]. Hence, it is necessary to explore an efficiently heterogeneous catalyst in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion for the practical applications.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) assembled from organic ligands and metal ions or clusters, aroused great attention owing to their designable structures, interesting properties and promising applications [23–34]. In particular, MOFs can serve as efficient catalysts for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation with epoxides and the terminal alkyne activation [35–39], exhibiting high catalytic performance and cyclicity. However, as far as we know, MOFs-based catalysts for aziridines with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition have been rarely reported [9–12].
In this contribution, three new MOFs, namely $\{[\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathbf{XN})_{2}$ - $\mathrm{(IPA)}_{2}]{\bullet}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\up;_{n}$ $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}_{0}$ (1), Mn (2), Ni (3), $\mathrm{XN=}4^{\prime}$ -(4-pyridine) $^{14,2^{\prime}:2^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -terpyridine, $\mathrm{{IPA}=}$ isophthalic acid, seen in Scheme 1) based on $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ as cluster notes have been obtained and structurally characterized. Compound 1 displays excellent solvent stability and acid/alkali tolerance in the $\mathsf{p H}$ range from 1 to 14. Catalytic investigations exhibit that compounds 1–3 can efficiently catalyze the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ transformation with aziridines into oxazolidinones under mild conditions and 1 can be reused at least five times without obvious decrease in catalytic activity.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and general methods
All the chemicals and solvents used in this work for crystal synthesis were purchases without further purification. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data were collected on a Rigaku D/Max-2500 diffractometer (Japan) using $\mathrm{{Cu}\ K a}$ radiation $(\lambda{=}1.54056\mathrm{\AA})$ in the $2\theta$ region from $3^{\circ}$ to $60^{\circ}$ . The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) were performed by a Bruker Tensor 27 spectrophotometer (Germany) as KBr pellets in the range of $400{\-}4000{\ c m}^{-1}$ (Figure S1, Supporting Information online). The data of element analyses (EA) for C, H, and N were recorded on Perkin-Elmer elemental analyzer. Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) were carried out by a Netzsch TG 209 TG-DTA analyzer (Germany) from 25 to $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere. The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra were measured by a Bruker 300 spectrometer (Germany) and $\mathrm{CDCl}_{3}$ $(7.26\mathrm{ppm})$ was used as internal reference. The content of metal was detected by $\scriptstyle[\mathbf{CP-9000(N+M)}$ spectrometric analysis.
## 2.2 Synthetic procedures
## 2.2.1 Synthesis of compound 1
Solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Co(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.0124\mathrm{g}$ , $0.05\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{\Omega}$ , IPA $(0.0083\ \mathrm{g},0.05\ \mathrm{mmol})$ and XN $(0.0062\mathrm{g}$ , $0.02\mathrm{mmol}_{,}^{\cdot}$ ) were dispersed in a mixture solvent of $N{,}N{\ }$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, $2{\mathrm{~mL}}$ ) and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ $\mathrm{8\mL}$ and were sealed in a $25~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel reaction vessel.
Then the reaction vessel was heated at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{d}$ and cooled to room temperature at $1.5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C/h}$ . Red block crystals of compound 1 were obtained ( $86\%$ yield based on $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}\bullet$ $\mathrm{4H_{2}O})$ ). Elemental analysis $(\%)$ Calcd for $\mathrm{C}_{28}\mathrm{H}_{20}\mathrm{CoN}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{5}$ : C 60.94, N 10.16, H 3.63. Found: $\mathrm{~C~}60.82,\mathrm{~N~}10.08,\mathrm{~H~}3.71$ (seen in Table S2, Supporting Information online).
## 2.2.2 Synthesis of compound 2
The synthesis of compound 2 is similar to that of 1, except that $\mathrm{Co(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is replaced by $\mathrm{Mn(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.0122\ {\mathrm{g}},$ $0.05\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{}$ . The mixture were sealed in a $25~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflonlined stainless steel autoclave and was heated at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{~d~}$ under autogenous pressure and then cooled gradually down to room temperature at the rate of $1.5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C/h}$ . Bright yellow block single crystals were collected (yield: $80\%$ ). Elemental analysis $(\%)$ for 2 $(\mathrm{C}_{28}\mathrm{H}_{20}\mathrm{MnN}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{5})$ , Calcd: C 61.38, H 3.65, N 10.23. Found: C 61.44, H 3.58, N 10.11 (seen in Table S2).
## 2.2.3 Synthesis of compound 3
The synthesis of compound 3 is similar to that of 1, except that $\mathrm{Co(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is replaced by $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.0124\ \mathrm{g}.$ $0.05\mathrm{mmol}_{,}^{\cdot}$ . The reactants were sealed in the same reaction container and were heated to $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ under autogenous pressure. After cooling to room temperature slowly, green block-shaped crystals of 3 were obtained (yield: $90\%$ ). Elemental analysis $(\%)$ for 3 $(\mathrm{C}_{28}\mathrm{H}_{20}\mathrm{NiN}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{5})$ ), Calcd: C 60.96, H 3.63, N 10.16. Found: C 60.86, H 3.57, N 10.09 (seen in Table S2).
## 2.3 Crystal structure determinations
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Description of crystal structure
Compounds 1–3 were prepared by $\mathrm{DMF/H_{2}O}$ mixed solution of XN, IPA and $\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M=Co}$ (1), Mn (2), Ni (3)) under solvothermal conditions. Single-crystal diffraction analyses reveal that compounds 1–3 all crystallize in the monoclinic system with the space group $C2/c$ . Because these three structures are isomorphous, the structure of compound 1 as a representative example is described in detail. As presented in Figure 1(a), the asymmetric unit of 1 is completed by one crystallographically independent ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , one independent IPA and one XN ligand. Each six-coordinated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ is connected by four carbonyl oxygen atoms (O1, O2, O3, O4) from three different IPA ligands and two $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ atoms (N1, N3) from two XN ligands, leading to an irregular octahedron coordination geometry. The $\mathrm{Co-O}$ and $\mathrm{Co-N}$ distances fall into the range of $2.026(3)–2.249(3)\AA$ and $2.136(4)-2.148(4)\mathrm{\AA}$ respectively, which matches well with those reported. Additionally, the $_{\mathrm{O-Co-O}}$ bond angles change from $59.90(10)^{\circ}$ to $151.08(11)^{\circ}$ , which agrees well with those reported previously (Table S1) [43,44].
In compound 1, two neighboring ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ are linked by two carboxylate ions to generate a $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{2}\right]^{2+}$ subunit. Meanwhile, the adjacent $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{2}\right]^{2+}$ units are connected by IPA ligands, leading to a 1D chain structure [45]. The 1D chain is pillared by the XN ligands, and the XN ligands distribute between the 1D chains in a parallel way, resulting in the formation of a 3D pillar-chain framework (Figure 1 (b)). In compound 1, a cavity with the pore of $3.74\times9.90\mathrm{\AA}^{2}$ can be observed along the $b$ direction, and the total solventaccessible volume calculated by PLATON software is only $4.5\%$ [42]. Another important structural feature of 1 is that it owns an array by the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions (the distances between ring centroids change from 3.619(2) to 3.869(2) Å, and the dihedral angles fall into the range of 0 to $3.245^{\circ}$ ). The large conjugated $\pi$ electron system plays an essential part in stabilizing the framework [46,47].
In order to get a deeper insight into the framework of 1, the structure is rationalized by the freely available TOPOS software package [48]. Then, each $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{2}\right]^{2+}$ unit can be treated as 4-connected node that links two XN and two IPA ligands, while each XN or IPA ligand belongs to a 2- connected spacer, which bridges two $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{2}\right]^{2+}$ units. Consequently, the 3D pillar-chain framework of 1 can be simplified into a 4-connected cds topology with the Schläfli symbol of $\{6^{\wedge}5;8\}$ (Figure 1(c, d)) [49].
## 3.2 PXRD and TGA analyses
The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) measurements have been performed for compounds 1–3 and experimental patterns agree well with the simulated ones, confirming the purity of 1–3 (Figure S2). To further explore the chemical stabilities of compound 1, the crystals were immersed in various acid/base solutions with $\mathrm{\pH}$ values ranging from $1-$ 14 and common organic solvents (acetone, $\mathrm{CCl}_{4},$ $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ , DMA, DMF, EAC, EtOH, $i{\mathrm{-PrOH}}$ , MeOH, BuOH) for $12\mathrm{h}$ , respectively. The agreement of treated patterns with the simulated peak positions implies that the structure of 1 still remain intact (Figures S3 and S4). The results mentioned above indicate that 1 owns excellent solvent stability and acid/base stability in the $\mathsf{p H}$ range of 1–14.
To study the thermal stability of compounds 1–3, the TGA measurements have been carried out in the temperature range of $30{-}700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under nitrogen, as presented in Figure S5. The weight loss of about $3.25\%$ can be observed from $100{-}300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (calculated $3.26\%$ , $3.29\%$ and $3.27\%$ , respectively), corresponding to the removal of two free water molecules. Subsequently, the frameworks of 1–3 start to decompose at about $425^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , demonstrating the high thermal stabilities of these three compounds.
## 3.3 Catalytic properties
In consideration of the unsaturated Lewis-acid active sites on the surface of these three compounds, 1–3 as catalysts have been employed in the cycloaddition of aziridines and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . To investigate the optimum conditions, 1-ethyl-2-phenylaziridine (1a) as a probe substrate reacts with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at different temperatures under $1\mathrm{MPa}$ with $5\mathrm{mol\%}$ tetrabutylammonium bromide (TBAB) as co-catalyst (Table 2, entries 1–4). As shown in Table 2, the desired product of 3- ethyl-5-phenyloxazolidin-2-one ${\bf(b)}$ has been gained with the yield of $89\%$ at $30{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (entry 1). With the temperature increasing, slight decreases of the yields for the corresponding oxazolidinones (b and c) have been observed. As a result, $30{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is chosen as the suitable temperature for this cycloaddition reaction, giving high yield and excellent selectivity toward b. Then, the effects of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressure, reaction time as well as the amount of catalyst and co-catalyst (TBAB) have been explored at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ As displayed in Table 2, the yield of desired product under 2 MPa $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is same as that with 1 MPa pressure (entry 5). However, the yield decreased to $19\%$ under $0.1\mathrm{MPa}$ (entry 6), which was clearly inferior the one under 1 MPa $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(89\%)$ , indicating that $1\mathrm{MPa}$ is the optimal $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressure. Moreover, the reaction yields all will decrease dramatically with the reduction of reaction time $(5\mathrm{h})$ (entry 7) or the amount of TBAB 1 $(2.5\mathrm{mol\%}$ , entry 8). With respect to the catalyst, the yield of desired product has no change when the amount of catalyst reaches to $40\mathrm{mg}$ (entry 9). However, only $32\%$ conversion to 4-phenyl-1,3-dioxolan-2-one was gotten by using $5\mathrm{mol\%}$ TBAB alone in the absence of catalyst at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (entry 14), revealing that the nucleophilic attack of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ to epoxide will be enhanced tremendously under the help of cobalt Lewisacid sites. As a consequence, the optimized reaction conditions in the cycloaddition of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and aziridines should be $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under 1 MPa with $20\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst 1 and $5\mathrm{mol\%}$ TBAB as co-catalyst, which is milder than that of the previously reported literatures. In previous work, when similar yield was obtained in comparison with compounds 1–3, $[\mathrm{Cu}_{30}]$ can catalyze the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ coupling with aziridines to oxazolidinones under the reaction conditions: $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $40\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst (based on metal center, about $5\mathrm{mol\%}$ ), $2\mathrm{MPa}$ , $32.4\mathrm{mg}$ TBAB, $12\mathrm{h}$ [9]. Moreover, the corresponding conditions of the catalytic reaction for $[Z\mathrm{n}_{24}]$ as catalyst is $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $2\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with $80~\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst and $32.4\mathrm{mg}$ co-catalyst (TBAB)
To further investigate and exemplify the catalytic performance of compound 1, several typical aziridines have been synthesized and employed as the substrates in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ transformation reaction (the detailed synthetic process of aziridines has been described in the Supporting Information online, Scheme 2). Under the optimized conditions, the cycloaddition reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with other aziridines substituted by different functional groups has been conducted and the corresponding oxazolidinones were listed in Table 3 (entries 1–5). The first three aziridines substituted by ethyl, propyl or butyl group at the nitrogen atom, respectively, were examined in the reaction. The aziridine with ethyl gives higher yield and better selectivity for products than the other two, possibly due to the lower steric hindrance of ethyl group (entry 1) than the propyl or butyl group (entries 2 and 3). In addition, ${\bf R}_{1}$ as ethyl group, 1-ethyl-2-(4-chlorophenyl)- aziridine and 1-ethyl-2-(4-methyl)-aziridine, bearing $-\mathrm{Cl}$ or $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ substituent group on phenyl ring, respectively, showed good catalytic activity in the cycloaddition reaction. Comparably, the yield of 3-ethyl-5-(4-chlorophenyl)oxazolidin2-one $(83\%)$ is lower than that of 3-ethyl-5-p-tolyloxazolidin-2-one, which might be ascribed to the electronwithdrawing effect of the chloride group on the phenyl ring. Therefore, compound 1 can be treated as excellent catalyst in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion with extensive aziridines into corresponding oxazolidinones under mild conditions.
Next, to explore the catalytic mechanism clearly, the influence of various cobalt sources on the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition with aziridine has been explored as control tests. Firstly, Co $(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CoSO_{4}}$ as catalysts replaced catalyst 1 to conduct the catalytic reaction, low yields were obtained with only $28\%$ , $25\%$ , $27\%$ and $29\%$ , respectively (entries 10–13, Table 2). The results above proved that both different inorganic ions and free ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions hardly catalyze the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ transformation reaction effectively. In contrast, when only TBAB was applied to catalyze the reaction, the yield $(32\%)$ is slightly higher than that under TBAB and Cosalts, which can be possibly ascribed to the interaction of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ with free ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to impede the nucleophilicity of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ ions. Interestingly, optimal yield of $89\%$ can be gained with the compound 1 serving as catalyst and TBAB as co-catalyst, displaying a significant enhancement than that of isolated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{{\bar{2}}+}$ ions and TBAB in catalytic reaction. The excellent catalytic property may be assigned to the following factors: (1) the electric neutrality and steric hindrance of the stable $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}$ unit in the framework effectively hinder the coordination interaction between $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to create more opportunities for the nucleophilic attack of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ towards aziridines; (2) the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ from TBAB and unsaturated Lewis-acid ${\bar{\mathbf{C}}}0^{2+}$ active sites from crystal 1 plays an important role in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ coupling reaction.
On the basis of previous reports [9–11], taking 1 as example, the possible mechanism for the conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and aziridines to form corresponding oxazolidinones has been postulated, as presented in Figure S8. First of all, the substrate (aziridines) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are concentrated on the surface of framework 1. Then, the substrate was activated through the coordination of unsaturated cobalt sites with the $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ atoms from the aziridines. Secondly, the ring-opening is started by the nucleophilic attack of $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ on the aziridines, leading two different paths a and b. Finally, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reacts with N ions of the ring-opened intermediate to generate carbamate salt and corresponding oxazolidinones have been obtained by intramolecular ring-closure. In terms of selectivity, path a belongs to the main process, producing the more stable carbamate salt intermediate due to ring-opening of the aziridine at the most substituted carbon. Therefore, catalyst 1 can catalyze the coupling reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and aziridine into desired oxazolidinone with excellent selectivity and high yield.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, three new 3D MOFs $\{\lbrack\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{XN})_{2}(\mathrm{IPA})_{2}\rbrack\bullet2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}_{0}$ (1), Mn (2), Ni (3)) were synthesized by solvothermal method, and their structures and properties have been investigated detailedly. It is proved that compound 1 features high thermal stability and excellent chemical stability in organic solvents and acid/base solutions. Notably, compounds 1–3 can be treated as efficient heterogeneous catalysts for the cycloaddition of aziridine and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at mild conditions, which is in accord with the atomic economy and green chemistry. Especially, compound 1 can be reused at least five times without any significant decrease in catalytic activity. Under the mildest conditions, 1 as a MOF-based catalyst presents high catalytic efficiency in the coupling reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and aziridines.
